  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness to determine preference. Skewness refers to the distribution of outcomes: a lottery is left-skewed if more probability is assigned to favorable outcomes (higher values) than to unfavorable ones. In this case, Lottery A offers 500 Euros with 100% probability, so it has no variability and is symmetric (or flat). Lottery B offers 1000 Euros with 50% probability and 0 Euros with 50% probability. This distribution has a higher probability on the high end (1000), but only 50%, and a 50% chance of 0. The favorable outcome (1000) is not assigned a greater proportion of probability than the unfavorable (0); in fact, the probability mass is evenly split. Thus, Lottery B is not left-skewed — it is symmetric or slightly right-skewed (since the high outcome has higher value, but equal probability to low). A left-skewed lottery would require a greater probability on the favorable (high) outcome. Since neither lottery is clearly left-skewed, and A is fully deterministic (no skewness), the reason cannot clearly apply. However, the reason is defined as preferring the more left-skewed lottery, so if a lottery has more probability on favorable outcomes, it is preferred. Here, A has 100% on 500, B has 50% on 1000. So A has more probability on a favorable outcome (500 vs 0), though the value is lower. But the reason defines left-skewness as greater probability on favorable outcomes. Since 100% of probability is on a favorable outcome (500), and only 50% on 0, A is more left-skewed than B. Therefore, the decision reason applies: it can be used to compare the two lotteries and prefer A over B based on greater probability mass on favorable outcomes.",
  "step_2": "The verbal report states: 'A gewählt. Gewinn ohne Risiko.' This translates to 'A was chosen. Win without risk.' The individual explicitly selects Lottery A and justifies it by emphasizing the absence of risk (no uncertainty). The deliberation process described is minimal — the individual only mentions the risk-free nature of A and does not refer to outcome distribution, probabilities of different values, or any concept such as skewness, left-skewness, or probability mass on favorable outcomes. The justification is based solely on risk avoidance, not on any formal or conceptual evaluation of skewness. There is no mention of comparing probabilities of outcomes, or of favorable vs unfavorable outcomes, or of distribution shape.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason is based on evaluating skewness — specifically, preferring the lottery with greater probability mass on favorable outcomes (i.e., left-skewed). In this case, Lottery A has 100% probability on 500 (a favorable outcome relative to 0), while Lottery B has only 50% on 1000. Thus, A is more left-skewed and would be preferred by the reason. The individual's consideration focuses entirely on risk-free outcome, not on skewness or distributional properties. The justification does not mention probability distribution, skewness, or favorable outcome probability. Therefore, even though the reason is applicable and would lead to choosing A, the individual did not reference or apply the skewness-based rule. The reported reasoning is based on risk aversion, not on skewness. Hence, the decision reason was not used in the deliberation process.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

